00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
all right  we are going to interrupt our regularly  scheduled retrospective on x-men and  talk about the  dexter series finale now i would have  had this video out  a little sooner to run the thing  actually aired except that when i first  tried to sit down and talk about the  thing  what the f so you know i took a few days  calmed down came back to try again  so i've let things simmer down  for a little bit and i'm gonna give it  another go  talking about the dexter's here  if you couldn't tell i was not exactly a  fan  it should be obvious that i'm gonna be  delving pretty heavily  into spoilers on this thing it's hard to  even know where to start  i'm gonna try and confine as much as i  can to  the series finale itself but i am gonna  have to talk  a little bit about the season leading up  to it so let's get that stuff out of the  way first  season eight has a couple of inherent  problems  the first is is that season two of  dexter really should have been the last  season  now when i say that i'm not saying they  should have stopped making more dexter  after the second season  what i'm saying is the feel of season  two  is what the feel of a final season  should have been for that show if you  don't remember  there was no season long serial killer  that dexter was hunting down  he was the killer and everybody was  closing in on him  oh my god they're gonna catch him that  should have been the feeling  for the final season another thing this  season have working against him this is  going to factor in more directly with  the  last episode is hannah mckay because the  idea  that dexter abandons harrison to  hannah mckay just seems  wrong here's the thing dexter may have  been a killer he may have  sort of been a magnet for all sorts of  dangerous stuff and he may have even  been  right in terms of he would always  endanger the people around him but at  least he knew how to function in society  hannah never really seems to have that  her solution to every problem she ever  has  is to poison it that's even how she  tries to get  dexter's attention when she wants his  help  what the heck is she gonna do the first  time harrison as a teenager  brings home a girl that she doesn't like  and then there's  deb i can't decide if i have an inherent  issue with deb dying or not  i do have an issue with how they did it  in terms of just  how it was portrayed and then how dexter  reacted which was just  weird he takes her body out and dumps it  in the ocean like he had done to all of  his victims  what in the hell is he doing and then  of course there's dexter himself now i  can just barely  go with the idea that he has come to the  conclusion  that he will always be a danger to those  around him and that he feels it's safest  to leave those people that he loves  behind i can kind of go with that  but it's still an inherently  unsatisfying thing for him to be doing  because he's been dealing with this  question in this issue  it's a legitimate fear but the idea that  he battled that  for eight seasons ultimately to just  give in  to that fear and say heck with it i'm  leaving  is supremely unsatisfying and it makes  him come across  as weak he basically gave up the fight  then we add in the whole thing with the  whole lumberjack thing which first of  all is weird in the first damn place  second of all there had just been some  hint that he was still  killing that you know his kill tools on  that table  something anything because if he's not  going to embrace  that dark passenger side of himself what  was the point of abandoning everybody  it's like they tried to make him a  martyr but all they really did was make  him a wuss  but you know what here's the thing all  of that all of those things are problems  they're  issues but it's the feel of the episode  it does not feel like dexter it feels  like  they wrote this thing handed it over to  a director and a crew  who had never seen an episode of dexter  in  their lives the beating heart  of dexter as a show has always been  tension the tension of him  hunting his next kill the tension that  maybe he's going to get caught  the tension that maybe he will get  killed on those occasions  when they had a really worthy adversary  for him it's always been about the  tension and there is almost  no tension whatsoever in the way this  final episode is presented  there's a split second where it starts  to feel like dexter again and that's  when dexter is sitting across from saxon  says  i'm gonna kill you with that pen for for  about  five seconds like oh and that's that's  the only fleeting moment and the rest of  it the thing just sort of  meanders about and not even tying up  loose ends i'm not even sure what it is  this episode is doing  because the actual events that had to be  wrapped up  get wrapped up by about the halfway mark  and then everything after that is just  what the  what what what is this i've  seen a lot of final episodes  but i have never ever  in my life an episode so completely  disappointing and disconnected from  everything that came before it maybe  once the rage comes down it'll be sort  of a fascinating case study  but i tell you right now  i got nothing on that  nihilistic little note this council  has adjourned

